  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 254 PM EDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\n- Dry though Saturday\n\nWe were originally a bit concerned about persistent low cloudiness \nlasting into this afternoon due to local observed and forecast \nsoundings indicating a deep and nearly saturated surface layer. \nHowever, formidable late August insolation is winning the day\ngrowing an even deeper mixed boundary layer. Recent cloud erosion\nover our central forecast area was impressively quick. This presumed\nlower tropospheric subsidence continues to be well sampled by \nGOES-E Lower Level Water Vapor (Channel 10) imagery. Moreover, we \nsaw slightly divergent surface flow per area observations earlier\ntoday and that may have been an additional mesoscale downward \nresponse in the wake of a subtle low level, low amplitude gravity\nwave that appears to have worked southeastward through the flow \nwhile creating local upticks and downticks in the depth and \ncoverage of stratocumulus observed this morning across much of \nLower MI.\n\nAs noted before, there are some model solutions, such as outlier \nREFS members, that pop up isolated showers generally along and \nsoutheast of a Lansing to Battle Creek line this afternoon. This\nis a low probability outcome (around 10 percent). Thunderstorms \nlook especially unlikely due to expected strengthening of the \nsubsidence inversion that will keep conditional instability \nconfined well beneath the ice production layer.\n\nFriday and Saturday still look dry with rather sharp upper shortwave \nridging upstream over the northern Plains and Upper Mississippi \nValley. Negative differential vorticity advection aloft downstream \nof this feature and over the western Great Lakes will promote \ncontinued deep tropospheric subsidence and surface pressure ridging.\n\n\n- Showers and thunderstorms Saturday night into next week\n\nOur greatest risk for significant weather beyond Saturday looks to \nbe Sunday night when there could be locally heavy rainfall. The\nexact corridor in which this occurs remains in question. The \nupper ridge will gradually deamplify while surface high pressure \nexits east of the area. A plume of anomalously large PWAT (90+ \npercentile) will approach from the west and overspread most of \nLower MI by Sunday evening. By Sunday night, medium range ensemble\nmean guidance develops a seasonably strong low-level jet (30+ \nknots at 850 mb and ~90 percentile) over the Midwest that should \nbe aimed towards Lower MI. This sets up a concern for locally \nheavy rainfall; accordingly, WPC does put our area in a Marginal \nrisk for excessive rainfall from 12Z Sunday through at least 12Z \nTuesday.\n\nPredictability really suffers after the expected round of \nprecipitation Sunday night into Monday as substantial convective \naugmentation of the synoptic flow will be possible. The best guess\nright now is continued unsettled conditions with episodic rainfall\nthrough Wednesday. In general terms, there does seem to be a trend\ntowards the reestablishment of upstream upper ridging starting \nWednesday night that will return subsidence and reduce PWAT \nmagnitudes to more seasonal levels.",
